READING DEX CODE UNITS WITHOUT CONFUSING THEM WITH ARM INSTRUCTIONS.

These are deliberately authored CODE-UNIT specimens with English
interpretations checked against the pinned source tables. They are not
captured output from an assembler, disassembler, ART run or physical device.
No claim is made that these snippets alone form complete valid DEX files.
Method prototypes, frames, metadata and handler tables remain necessary.

SPECIMEN ONE: AN INTEGER ADDITION.

Assume a static method returning int with at least three registers.
Its instruction code units, in offset order, are:
  index 0: 7012
  index 1: 3112
  index 2: 0290
  index 3: 0100
  index 4: 020f

The low byte 12 identifies const/4. At index zero, the high nibbles mean
literal seven and destination v0. At index one they mean literal three
and destination v1. Index two begins add-int, format 23x. Its high byte
selects destination v2; the following unit supplies source B=v0 in its
low byte and source C=v1 in its high byte. Read seven and three, retain
their thirty-two-bit sum ten and write v2. Next execute index four,
return v2. The return ends this invocation; it does not fetch index five.

The unit 0100 at index three is an OPERAND of add-int. Looking at it
without the preceding instruction boundary could misleadingly resemble
a packed-switch payload identifier. It is not a payload at this location.

SPECIMEN TWO: DIVISION AND OVERFLOW.

The two units 0293 0100 identify div-int v2, v0, v1. Assume v0 contains
0x80000000 and v1 contains 0xffffffff, interpreted as minus 2³¹ and minus
one. The result in v2 is 0x80000000 and normal continuation advances by
two units. No signed-overflow exception occurs. If v1 instead contains
zero, no normal quotient is supplied and exception dispatch begins.
This is a stated source-semantic witness, not an executed arithmetic oracle.

SPECIMEN THREE: THE SAME NOP BYTE DOES NOT GRANT PAYLOAD EXECUTION.

A packed switch at index zero can begin with 002b 0004 0000.
Its table is at index four, since its signed displacement is four.
Index three may contain 000e, a return-void for the unmatched path.
An aligned one-case table beginning at index four can be:
  0100 0001 0007 0000 0003 0000

Identifier 0100 and count one introduce first key seven and target three.
The target is index THREE, relative to the switch at zero, not index seven
relative to the payload at four. Both a match and a miss in this deliberately
simple specimen reach the return at index three. The table is never fetched
as an instruction. Changing it to begin at an odd code-unit offset violates
its alignment requirement even though all the listed halfwords still fit.

An empty sparse table is 0200 0000 and occupies two code units.
A byte-array payload for three elements 11,22,33 in hexadecimal is
  0300 0001 0003 0000 2211 0033
and occupies six code units. The high zero byte of its final unit is
padding, not a fourth element. Neither specimen is a standard extra opcode
with a primary byte greater than 0xff.

SPECIMEN FOUR: A RESULT MUST HAVE ITS PRODUCER.

If a valid invocation returning int completes normally, the immediately
following move-result may read its pending value. Branching directly to
that same instruction does not create a value. Similarly move-exception
requires handler entry; a decoder's ability to print the mnemonic does
not establish that a normal branch may enter it.

HOW TO INSPECT REAL COMPILER OUTPUT.

The canonical android-NDK DEX backend retains .checked.anf, .dex.plan,
.smali and .dex files beside one another. Begin with the actual .dex file's
version and code_item, then establish instruction boundaries with a decoder
qualified for that version. Preserve its exact name/version and original
output. Match the numerical opcode to this catalogue; preserve operands,
code-unit offsets, referenced tables, branch targets and payload extents.
Do not infer the supported ISA from only the mnemonics that this compiler
currently emits. Do not describe the backend's .smali plan rendering as an
independent disassembly of its emitted .dex bytes.

The independent dexlib2 enum frozen with this catalogue proves a second
source mapping, not that a decoder executable was run here. An eventual
captured-byte example must name the exact producing artifact, its digest,
the decoder identity, and what actually ran. Existing ARM byte witnesses
remain unchanged and are not relabeled as DEX evidence.
